package com.hpcai270

// Days 16–50: foundation-to-admin bridge.
// Days 16–25 are expanded multi-page lessons; later days remain the next expansion batch.
val hpcDaysPart4 = listOf(
    Day(16,"Linux for HPC","The operating system","Linux is the control surface of modern HPC infrastructure. Compute nodes, storage servers, network tools, schedulers and GPU software are commonly administered from Linux.","WHY IT MATTERS\n\nAn HPC storage engineer does not only manage a storage array. You may inspect a client, kernel, network interface, filesystem, process and storage target before deciding where the fault lives.","MENTAL MODEL\n\nAPPLICATION\n ↓\nLIBRARIES / FILESYSTEM\n ↓\nKERNEL\n ↓\nDRIVER\n ↓\nDEVICE / NETWORK\n\nEvery layer can affect I/O.","CORE ADMIN AREAS\n\nProcesses • CPU • memory • filesystems • devices • networking • services • logs • permissions • automation\n\nLearn the layer before memorizing the command.","COMMAND STARTER\n\nuname -a\nlsblk\nfindmnt\nps aux\nip addr\nip route\ndf -h\nfree -h\n\nRun each and explain what question it answers.","STORAGE-ADMIN CONNECTION\n\nIf a user says ‘storage is slow’, do not immediately blame storage. Check client CPU/memory, mount state, network health, filesystem behavior and device latency.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: build a Linux VM and inventory CPU, RAM, disks, mounts and NICs.\n\nInterview: ‘A storage mount is slow. What Linux evidence would you collect first?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: draw the application-to-device path and name the Linux layer you would inspect next."),

    Day(17,"Linux filesystem","Paths, mounts and devices","Linux presents storage through a filesystem namespace. A block device can contain a filesystem, and that filesystem becomes usable at a mount point.","THE KEY DISTINCTION\n\nDEVICE ≠ FILESYSTEM ≠ MOUNT POINT\n\nExample: a disk/partition may hold an ext4 or XFS filesystem, which can then be mounted at /data.","ANALOGY\n\nDevice = warehouse building\nFilesystem = organization inside it\nMount point = street address where you enter it\n\nThe same physical resource is useful only when the OS can access it correctly.","PATH\n\n/dev/nvme0n1\n   ↓\npartition / namespace\n   ↓\nfilesystem\n   ↓\n/data\n\nNetwork filesystems add another layer: server + network + client mount.","TOOLS\n\nlsblk -f\nfindmnt\ndf -hT\nmount\nblkid\n\nUse df for filesystem space and lsblk for block-device topology; they answer different questions.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\n‘Disk exists but application cannot write’ → check mount state, filesystem permissions, read-only state, free space and errors.\n\nDo not confuse ‘device visible’ with ‘application storage available’.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: create a small filesystem in a VM, mount it at /data, inspect it with lsblk/df/findmnt, then unmount it.\n\nInterview: ‘What is the difference between /dev/nvme0n1 and /data?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow we move from storage objects to running workloads."),

    Day(18,"Linux processes","What is running?","A process is an executing program with its own resources and state. HPC administrators inspect processes to understand workload behavior, resource pressure and hangs.","PROCESS VIEW\n\nUSER → SHELL → PROCESS → SYSTEM CALLS → KERNEL\n\nA storage command is still a process. Its CPU, memory, open files and network activity can matter when diagnosing I/O.","ANALOGY\n\nThink of an HPC node as a workshop. Processes are workers. CPU cores are workbenches, memory is workspace and storage/network are supply routes. Too many workers competing for the same route creates contention.","PROCESS STATES\n\nR = running/runnable\nS = sleeping\nD = uninterruptible sleep, often seen during I/O waits\nZ = zombie\n\nA D-state process is especially interesting during storage troubleshooting because it may be waiting on kernel I/O.","COMMANDS\n\nps aux\nps -ef\ntop\nhtop\npgrep <name>\nkill <pid>\n\nLearn to identify PID, CPU%, memory%, state and command line.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nIf applications hang, correlate process state with disk/network behavior. High CPU can indicate compute pressure; D-state processes can indicate I/O waits; many runnable processes can indicate CPU contention.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: run a CPU or I/O workload and watch it with top. Find its PID and inspect it with ps.\n\nInterview: ‘Why would a storage engineer care about Linux processes?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: explain how an application becomes an observable process and why process state is evidence."),

    Day(19,"Linux memory","RAM, cache and pressure","Memory is working space for applications and the operating system. Linux also uses RAM as page cache, which can make repeated file reads appear much faster than the underlying storage device.","I/O PATH\n\nAPPLICATION\n ↓\nPAGE CACHE\n ↓\nFILESYSTEM\n ↓\nBLOCK LAYER\n ↓\nDEVICE\n\nA benchmark that accidentally measures cache is not measuring the same thing as direct device I/O.","ANALOGY\n\nRAM is a desk; storage is a filing cabinet. If the document is already on the desk, reading it is fast. If it must be fetched from the cabinet, the access path is slower.","MEMORY SIGNALS\n\nfree -h\nvmstat 1\n\nWatch available memory, swap activity and system behavior. Memory pressure can indirectly change I/O performance through reclaim and cache behavior.","PERFORMANCE CONNECTION\n\nTwo identical reads can have different latency: first read may fetch from storage; later read may be served from cache. This is why controlled benchmarking needs a defined workload and methodology.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nSymptoms: swapping, memory exhaustion, unexpected latency.\n\nQuestions: Is the workload memory-bound? Is swap active? Is the test cached? Are multiple jobs competing for RAM?\n\nDo not interpret one metric in isolation.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: run a file read twice and observe system behavior; inspect memory with free -h and vmstat.\n\nInterview: ‘Why can page cache make a storage test misleading?’\n\nNEXT LINK: permissions determine who can use the filesystem once it is mounted."),

    Day(20,"Linux permissions","Users, groups and access","HPC systems are shared environments. Files and directories need correct ownership and permissions so users and services can access data without exposing it incorrectly.","BASIC MODEL\n\nUSER → GROUP → MODE\n\nRead (r) • write (w) • execute (x)\n\nFor directories, execute also controls traversal through the directory.","ANALOGY\n\nA building can be readable, writable and enterable by different groups of people. File permissions are the access-control signs on each door.","EXAMPLE\n\n-rw-r-----\n\nowner: read/write\ngroup: read\nothers: none\n\nNumeric permissions such as 640 encode the same idea in compact form.","COMMANDS\n\nls -l\nstat file\nwhoami\nid\nchmod\nchown\n\nUnderstand ownership before changing it. In production, permission changes can have application impact.","STORAGE CONNECTION\n\nA healthy NFS or parallel filesystem can still look ‘broken’ to a user if UID/GID mapping, ownership or permissions prevent access. Access control is part of storage troubleshooting.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: create two users/groups in a VM, create a shared directory and test read/write access.\n\nInterview: ‘Why can a user see a directory but still fail to open a file?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: read ls -l output and reason about owner/group/other access."),

    Day(21,"Linux networking","Interfaces, IPs and routes","Before learning RDMA and InfiniBand, master ordinary Linux networking. An interface connects the host to a network; an IP identifies an endpoint; a route determines where packets should go.","HOST NETWORK MODEL\n\nAPPLICATION\n ↓\nSOCKET\n ↓\nTCP/UDP\n ↓\nIP ROUTING\n ↓\nNIC\n ↓\nSWITCH / FABRIC\n\nHigh-performance networking builds on these fundamentals.","ANALOGY\n\nIP address = house address\nRoute = road map\nNIC = vehicle\nSwitch = intersection system\n\nIf the address or route is wrong, faster roads do not help.","COMMANDS\n\nip addr\nip link\nip route\nss -tulpn\nping <host>\n\nLearn to distinguish link state, IP configuration, routing and application listening state.","STORAGE CONNECTION\n\nNFS, S3, NVMe/TCP and management APIs depend on networking. A storage service can be healthy while the client path is broken.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nLayer 1/2: is the interface up?\nLayer 3: is the IP/route correct?\nLayer 4: is the port reachable/listening?\nApplication: is the service responding correctly?\n\nThis layered method scales into RDMA troubleshooting.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: inspect ip addr and ip route on your VM. Find its default route and active interface.\n\nInterview: ‘NFS mount fails. What network evidence would you collect?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow we examine how Linux records failures and events."),

    Day(22,"Linux logs","Evidence before guessing","Logs provide evidence about kernels, devices, services, authentication, filesystems and networking. Good troubleshooting correlates events by time instead of relying on assumptions.","EVIDENCE CHAIN\n\nSYMPTOM\n ↓\nTIMESTAMP\n ↓\nKERNEL / SERVICE LOG\n ↓\nRELATED HOST / NETWORK EVENTS\n ↓\nHYPOTHESIS\n ↓\nTEST\n\nThis is the foundation of disciplined troubleshooting.","ANALOGY\n\nA flight recorder is more useful than a passenger saying ‘the plane felt strange’. Logs are the infrastructure flight recorder—imperfect, but evidence.","COMMON SOURCES\n\njournalctl\ndmesg\n/var/log/*\n\nExamples include kernel messages, service logs and authentication records. Exact paths vary by distribution and configuration.","WHAT TO CORRELATE\n\nTime of failure\nHost\nProcess/service\nDevice\nNetwork interface\nFilesystem\n\nFor distributed storage, compare client and server timelines when possible.","TROUBLESHOOTING EXAMPLE\n\nUser reports an I/O hang at 10:14. Check client process state and kernel messages around 10:14, then inspect network/storage-side evidence. The goal is to narrow the fault domain before changing anything.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: use journalctl and dmesg on your VM. Identify one boot event and one device/network message.\n\nInterview: ‘Why are timestamps important in distributed-system troubleshooting?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: build a simple evidence timeline from Linux logs."),

    Day(23,"Bash basics","The administrator's automation language","Bash lets infrastructure engineers combine small Unix tools into repeatable workflows. You do not need to become a software engineer before you can automate useful storage operations.","THE PIPELINE IDEA\n\nCOMMAND → PIPE → FILTER → TRANSFORM → REPORT\n\nExamples:\nlsblk | grep nvme\njournalctl | grep -i error\n\nPipelines turn raw output into evidence.","ANALOGY\n\nImagine a factory conveyor: each station performs one simple job, then passes the result to the next station. Unix commands work similarly.","CORE TOOLS\n\nls\ncat\ngrep\nawk\nsed\nsort\nuniq\ncut\ntee\nwc\n\nLearn one useful task for each instead of memorizing syntax without a purpose.","SAFE ADMIN HABITS\n\nUse clear variables. Quote paths. Check assumptions. Prefer read-only inspection first. Test destructive commands in a lab. Print meaningful output when scripts are used operationally.","STORAGE USE CASES\n\nCollect mount status across hosts. Search logs for I/O errors. Parse benchmark output. Inventory devices. Generate a health report.\n\nAutomation turns repeated manual checks into consistent procedures.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: create a text file with fake storage alerts and use grep/sort/uniq to count alert types.\n\nInterview: ‘Why is a pipeline useful for infrastructure troubleshooting?’\n\nNEXT LINK: tomorrow you turn commands into a reusable script."),

    Day(24,"Bash scripting","Repeatable operations","A Bash script packages commands and logic into a repeatable procedure. Reliable scripts validate inputs, handle errors and make their output understandable.","SCRIPT FLOW\n\nINPUT\n ↓\nVALIDATE\n ↓\nCOLLECT\n ↓\nCHECK\n ↓\nREPORT\n\nFor operational automation, predictability is more important than cleverness.","ANALOGY\n\nA runbook is a recipe for a human. A script is a recipe the computer can execute. Both should state assumptions and expected outcomes.","ESSENTIAL CONCEPTS\n\n#!/bin/bash\nvariables\nif statements\nloops\nexit codes\nfunctions\n\nA script should fail clearly when an important prerequisite is missing.","MINI PROJECT\n\nCreate a script that checks whether /data is mounted.\n\nQuestions to answer:\n• Does the mount exist?\n• Is it writable?\n• How much space remains?\n\nKeep the first version read-only.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nWhen a script behaves unexpectedly, print variables, inspect exit codes and test each command independently. Avoid hiding errors with overly broad error suppression.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: write and run the /data mount-check script in your VM. Then intentionally test it when /data is absent.\n\nInterview: ‘What makes an infrastructure script safe?’\n\nYOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO: automate a small diagnostic without blindly running destructive commands."),

    Day(25,"Python basics","Automation beyond Bash","Python becomes useful when infrastructure automation needs structured data, APIs, JSON, larger logic or reusable modules. For this course, the goal is practical infrastructure automation—not becoming a full-time application developer.","WHERE PYTHON FITS\n\nBASH → quick host commands\nPYTHON → structured automation\n\nPython can read files, parse JSON, call APIs, process command output and produce reports.","ANALOGY\n\nBash is a multitool: excellent for quick jobs. Python is a workshop: more setup, but better when the job has many moving parts.","CORE CONCEPTS\n\nvariables\nstrings/lists/dictionaries\nconditions\nloops\nfunctions\nfiles\nJSON\nexceptions\n\nThese concepts are enough to begin infrastructure automation.","STORAGE EXAMPLE\n\nA Python tool could read a list of storage endpoints, run a health check, collect latency/capacity information and produce structured JSON or CSV output. Later, APIs can replace shell commands.","TROUBLESHOOTING + REAL WORLD\n\nAutomation can fail because of permissions, unavailable hosts, malformed data, timeouts or unexpected command output. Good scripts report which target failed and why.\n\nThis becomes valuable when managing many HPC nodes or storage targets.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: write a Python program that stores three storage device names in a list and prints them. Then use a dictionary to attach a capacity value to each.\n\nInterview: ‘When would you choose Python instead of Bash?’\n\nCHECKPOINT: You now have the Linux + automation foundation needed for deeper HPC storage administration. Next lessons will connect these skills to block, file and object storage."),

    Day(26,"Storage protocols","Block, file and object","Storage can be exposed as block, file or object. The interface determines how applications address data and what the administrator must troubleshoot.","BLOCK → device/volume\nFILE → path/filesystem\nOBJECT → object + metadata + API\n\nExamples: FC/iSCSI/NVMe-oF; NFS/SMB/Lustre; S3.","REAL-WORLD MAP\n\nEnterprise SAN → block\nNAS → file\nCloudian → S3 object\nLustre/Storage Scale → parallel file\n\nThe technologies differ, but all ultimately move and persist data.","DECISION RULE\n\nNeed a raw volume? Think block.\nNeed shared POSIX paths? Think file.\nNeed API-driven scalable objects? Think object.\nNeed massive concurrent HPC file access? Think parallel filesystem.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nBlock: path/volume/multipath/latency.\nFile: mount/export/permissions/network/filesystem.\nObject: endpoint/authentication/API/bucket/object semantics.\n\nThe interface changes the diagnostic workflow.","AI CONNECTION\n\nAI pipelines commonly combine local NVMe, shared high-performance file storage and object storage. Learning the differences prevents choosing the wrong tool for the workload.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: draw one architecture for block, one for NFS and one for S3.\n\nInterview: ‘Why isn't S3 simply another mounted filesystem?’\n\nNEXT: NFS gives you the shared-file foundation before parallel filesystems."),
    Day(27,"NFS","Shared file storage","NFS lets Linux clients access files from a remote server using a network filesystem protocol. It is essential background for understanding more specialized shared filesystems.","PATH\n\nAPPLICATION → VFS → NFS CLIENT → NETWORK → NFS SERVER → LOCAL STORAGE\n\nThe application sees a filesystem path, but the data may be remote.","ANALOGY\n\nA local filing cabinet becomes a shared office filing room. Users still see folders and files, but access now depends on the network and server.","KEY CONCEPTS\n\nExports\nmounts\nUID/GID\npermissions\nNFS versions\nserver availability\nnetwork dependency\n\nLearn the concepts before memorizing mount options.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nMount fails → DNS/network/export/access.\nMount works but access fails → permissions/identity.\nI/O is slow → client/server load, network latency, filesystem and backend storage.\n\nAlways separate availability from performance.","AI/HPC CONNECTION\n\nNFS is common for general shared data and management workloads, but very large parallel AI/HPC workloads may need parallel filesystems designed for high concurrency and bandwidth.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: if you have two Linux VMs, configure a small NFS share and mount it from the client.\n\nInterview: ‘What extra failure domain does NFS introduce compared with local storage?’\n\nNEXT: object storage changes the access model from filesystem paths to APIs."),
    Day(28,"S3 object storage","Buckets, keys and APIs","S3 is an object-storage API model. Data is stored as objects with metadata and addressed through buckets and keys rather than traditional POSIX filesystem paths.","MODEL\n\nAPPLICATION\n ↓ HTTPS / S3 API\nBUCKET\n ↓\nOBJECT + METADATA\n\nThe application asks the object service to PUT, GET, LIST or DELETE objects.","ANALOGY\n\nA warehouse with labeled boxes. The box is the object; the label is the key; the warehouse section is the bucket. You request boxes through an API instead of walking a filesystem tree.","WHY IT SCALES\n\nObject systems can distribute data across many nodes and media. They are well suited to large datasets, backups, archives and data lakes where API access is acceptable.","REAL PRODUCTS\n\nCloudian HyperStore provides S3-compatible object storage. Public cloud providers also expose object storage services. Compatibility does not mean identical performance, features or operational behavior.","AI CONNECTION + TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nAI datasets may originate in object storage before being staged into faster training storage. Diagnose endpoint reachability, credentials, bucket/key behavior, API errors, throughput and application concurrency.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: use a local S3-compatible lab such as MinIO if available; create a bucket and upload/download an object.\n\nInterview: ‘When would you prefer S3 over NFS?’\n\nNEXT: tomorrow we examine RAID and the difference between redundancy and performance."),
    Day(29,"RAID concepts","Protection versus performance","RAID combines drives to provide different combinations of capacity, redundancy and performance. RAID level is a design decision, not a universal performance guarantee.","CORE LEVELS\n\nRAID 0 → stripe, no redundancy\nRAID 1 → mirror\nRAID 5/6 → parity\nRAID 10 → mirror + stripe\n\nActual behavior depends on workload, controller/software and media.","ANALOGY\n\nRAID 1 is like keeping two identical copies. RAID 0 is splitting a job among workers without a backup. RAID 10 combines duplication with parallelism.","WHY HPC ENGINEERS CARE\n\nProtection affects usable capacity, rebuild behavior and write overhead. HPC storage may also use distributed protection mechanisms above individual drives, so do not assume every modern system is ‘just RAID’.","FAILURE THINKING\n\nDrive failure → degraded state → rebuild/recovery → performance impact.\n\nA healthy system must be designed not only for normal operation but also for failure and recovery behavior.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nCheck failed/degraded members, rebuild activity, media errors and latency. During recovery, understand whether the workload and protection layer are competing for resources.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: calculate usable capacity for simple RAID 0/1/5/6/10 examples on paper.\n\nInterview: ‘Why can a redundant RAID layout still have poor performance?’\n\nNEXT: we now quantify storage performance using IOPS, throughput and latency."),
    Day(30,"Storage performance","IOPS, throughput and latency","Storage performance has several dimensions. A good engineer identifies the workload before interpreting a number.","THREE CORE METRICS\n\nIOPS → operations/second\nTHROUGHPUT → bytes/second\nLATENCY → time per operation\n\nBlock size, read/write mix, queue depth, concurrency and working-set behavior all matter.","ANALOGY\n\nIOPS = how many customers a shop serves.\nThroughput = how much cargo moves.\nLatency = how long one customer waits.\n\nA shop can be excellent at one metric and poor at another.","WORKLOAD EXAMPLES\n\nSmall random I/O → often latency/IOPS sensitive.\nLarge sequential I/O → often bandwidth sensitive.\nMetadata-heavy workload → filesystem metadata performance matters.\nAI training → aggregate data-feed rate and concurrency matter.","MEASUREMENT RULE\n\nNever say ‘this storage is fast’ without defining: block size, read/write ratio, concurrency, queue depth, client count, duration and measurement location.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nHigh latency with low throughput may indicate a bottleneck or small I/O. High throughput with poor application performance can mean the application is waiting on a different layer. Measure end-to-end, then isolate layers.","HANDS-ON + INTERVIEW\n\nLab: write three example workloads and predict which metric matters most.\n\nInterview: ‘Can a system have higher IOPS but lower throughput?’ Yes—especially when I/O sizes differ.\n\nCHECKPOINT: Days 16–30 built the Linux, networking, automation and storage-performance foundation for the upcoming HPC labs.")
)
